          "type": "turn",
          "question": "How is COPD different from asthma?",
          "answer": "The search results do not explain the main differences between COPD and asthma, such as causes, typical age of onset, or whether the lung changes can be reversed. I can't fill that gap from memory, so please check a trusted medical source or ask a clinician.\n\nHere is what the results do say:\n\n- **COPD** is chronic obstructive pulmonary disease. The COPD summary (condition-copd) says care includes a written plan for flare-ups (called exacerbations) and treatment of conditions that come with it, such as heart disease. It does not say how COPD differs from asthma.\n- **Asthma and COPD can both be present.** The asthma coding summary (coding-asthma-j45) and the COPD coding summary (coding-copd-j44) both say that when a record documents both, they are reported together. Neither one is chosen over the other.\n- **Both can cause sudden severe breathlessness.** The severe breathing difficulty guidance (red-flag-severe-breathing-difficulty) lists asthma attack and COPD exacerbation among the causes.\n\n**Get urgent help now if someone has:** breathlessness that doesn't ease with their usual reliever inhaler, breathlessness at rest, confusion or unusual drowsiness, blue lips or fingers, or chest pain. Call emergency services. The severe breathing difficulty guidance says to keep the person sitting upright and to use any reliever or emergency medicine as their own action plan directs.\n\nA clinician can explain which condition applies to you and what treatment fits.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
